# The Olympics and Multi-Sport Events: From Athens 1896 to the Present

> The modern Olympics were revived in 1896 by a French baron who
> believed international sport could prevent war. It could not — but
> it created the world's most examined sporting dataset. India's own
> Olympic story is a hockey dynasty, a long wait for individual
> medals, and a new century of shooters, wrestlers, badminton
> players, and javelin throwers. Every general-awareness paper asks
> from this history.

## 1. The Ancient and Modern Games

[definition]
The **ancient Olympic Games** were held at Olympia, Greece, from 776
BCE (traditionally) for over a millennium. The **modern Olympics**
were revived by **Pierre de Coubertin**, with the first Games at
**Athens in 1896**. The Olympic symbol — five interlaced rings —
represents the union of the five inhabited continents, and the motto
is *Citius, Altius, Fortius* ("Faster, Higher, Stronger"), extended
in 2021 with a fourth word: *– Communiter* ("together").
[/definition]

The rings' colours — blue, yellow, black, green, and red on a white
field — were chosen because every national flag of the era contained
at least one of them. The International Olympic Committee (IOC) sits
in **Lausanne, Switzerland**. The Games are quadrennial; the Winter
Olympics split from the Summer calendar after 1992 and now fall two
years apart (e.g., Tokyo 2020/2021 summer, Beijing 2022 winter).

## 2. India at the Olympics

| Milestone | Person / Team | Games |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First participation | Norman Pritchard (2 athletics silvers) | Paris 1900 |
| First team participation & first gold | Hockey team (Dhyan Chand's era begins) | Amsterdam 1928 |
| First individual medal (independent India) | K.D. Jadhav — wrestling bronze | Helsinki 1952 |
| First individual silver | Rajyavardhan Singh Rathore — shooting | Athens 2004 |
| First individual gold | Abhinav Bindra — 10 m air rifle | Beijing 2008 |
| First athletics gold (post-independence) | Neeraj Chopra — javelin | Tokyo 2020 (held 2021) |
| First woman medallist | Karnam Malleswari — weightlifting bronze | Sydney 2000 |
| First boxing medal | Vijender Singh — bronze | Beijing 2008 |
| First badminton medal | Saina Nehwal — bronze | London 2012 |
| First athletics silver (woman) | P.V. Sindhu — badminton silver | Rio 2016 |
| Two medals at one Games (post-independence) | Manu Bhaker — shooting bronze (individual + mixed team) | Paris 2024 |

India's hockey team won **eight Olympic golds** — 1928 through 1980
— still the record for any team in any Olympic sport. The 1928–56
run of six consecutive golds is a standalone exam fact. Dhyan Chand
scored 14 goals in the 1928 Games alone; National Sports Day (29
August) is his birthday.

The Tokyo 2020 Games (held in 2021, and papers use both names) were
India's best-ever haul with **seven medals**, including Neeraj
Chopra's javelin gold — independent India's first athletics gold —
and a hockey bronze after 41 years. At **Paris 2024**, India won six
medals; Neeraj Chopra took silver in the javelin, and **Manu Bhaker
became the first Indian to win two medals at a single post-1947
Olympics**.

## 3. The Asian and Commonwealth Games

The **Asian Games** (Asiad) began in **1951 at New Delhi**, which
hosted them again in **1982** — the only city to host the Asiad
twice until Hangzhou 2023's record-breaking edition. India's Asiad
best came at home in 1951 (second in medals) and again in recent
editions where the athletics contingent led the charge. The Olympic
Council of Asia sits in Kuwait.

The **Commonwealth Games** began in **1930 at Hamilton, Canada**,
under the name British Empire Games. India has hosted once — **Delhi
2010** — where India finished second in the medal table, its best
ever, with shooters leading the tally.

| Event | First held | India's headline |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Modern Olympics | Athens 1896 | 35 medals overall, incl. 10 golds |
| Asian Games | New Delhi 1951 | Host twice (1951, 1982) |
| Commonwealth Games | Hamilton 1930 | Host 2010; best finish 2nd |
| FIFA World Cup | 1930 | India qualified 1950 but withdrew |
| Cricket World Cup | 1975 | Champions 1983, 2011 |
| T20 World Cup | 2007 | Champions 2007, 2024 |
| Hockey World Cup | 1971 | Champions 1975 |

## 4. World Cups and the Sports-Calendar Cornerstones

India's cricket World Cup history compresses to two dates exams use:
**1983** (Kapil Dev's team, at Lord's, beating the West Indies) and
**2011** (M.S. Dhoni's team, at Wankhede, beating Sri Lanka). India
also won the **inaugural T20 World Cup in 2007** (Johannesburg) and
the 2024 edition in the Americas. For football, the facts are fewer
but reliable: **Brazil holds five FIFA World Cup titles**, the
tournament began in 1930 (Uruguay, winners at home), and India
qualified for the **1950 World Cup but withdrew** — famously over
the barefoot-boots rule dispute, though finances and priorities also
played their part.

In hockey's World Cup (since 1971), India lifted the trophy once —
**Kuala Lumpur 1975**, captained by Ajit Pal Singh. The 2023 edition
was hosted by Odisha (Rourkela and Bhubaneswar), a current-affairs
crossover with the "sports venues" question set.

[example]
The classic sequence trap: "India's first Olympic individual gold
was won by — (a) the hockey team 1928, (b) K.D. Jadhav 1952, (c)
Abhinav Bindra 2008, (d) Neeraj Chopra 2021." Two qualifiers matter:
*individual* (excluding the hockey team) and *gold* (excluding
Jadhav's bronze and Rathore's silver). The answer is Bindra, 2008.
Neeraj Chopra's gold came later and — the follow-up — was India's
first athletics gold, a different superlative again.
[/example]

[summary]
Framework facts: ancient Olympia from 776 BCE; revival by Coubertin
at Athens 1896; five rings for five continents; motto extended with
"Communiter" in 2021; IOC at Lausanne. India: Pritchard 1900 (first
participation), hockey gold 1928 beginning an eight-gold dynasty,
Jadhav 1952 (first individual medal), Bindra 2008 (first individual
gold), Neeraj Chopra 2021 (first athletics gold), Malleswari 2000
(first woman medallist), Tokyo 2020 the best haul with seven, Manu
Bhaker 2024 the first double-medallist of the post-independence era.
Around the Olympics: Asian Games from New Delhi 1951, Commonwealth
Games from Hamilton 1930 (Delhi 2010 host, second place), cricket
World Cups 1983 and 2011, T20 titles 2007 and 2024, hockey's only
World Cup in 1975.
[/summary]
